  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A rather pleasant day across the region as a weak \nstationary boundary is beginning to drift northward. Weak \nconvergence along this boundary will keep a narrow corridor of mid \nand upper 50 dewpoints drifting northward. Elsewhere, as afternoon \nmixing and heating take over, we should see dew points mix downwards \nslightly.\n\nTONIGHT: A beautiful evening can be expected through the region as \nhigh level cirrus pushes southeast.  Winds will stay light and mixy \nthrough the night as the aforementioned front lifts northeast. While \nsome valley areas may cool into the 50s, the prevailing overnight \nlow temperatures will stay in the lower 60s in many areas, only a \nfew degrees off typical seasonal high temperatures.\n\nSATURDAY: Near to record high temperatures will arrive for Saturday, \naccompanied by gusty southwesterly winds and plenty of dry air.\nThe strong surface pressure gradient already pushing 15 to 20 \nmph sustained winds by mid-morning will continue to tighten \nthrough the afternoon, but the deepening mixing will promote \nmix down of the 40+ knot LLJ that will persist into the mid-\nlate afternoon hours. In these situations historically, deeper \nmixing models will verify better for dew points and winds, so \nhave mixed both the HRRR/RAP into the forecast through the \nafternoon hours. This will promote occasional wind gusts of 35 \nto 45 mph (highest along the Buffalo Ridge), and dew points \ndropping into the lower 50s. While the NBM has started to catch-\nup to recent warm temperature bias, making the mean population a\nbit more reasonable, have again bumped up temperatures slightly\ntowards the 90th percentile given synoptic potential. This will\nplace high temperatures near daily records. One thing to watch \nfor Saturday will be the potential for harvest related blowing \ndust, which could produce some very localized visibility and AQI\nreductions. The combination of heat, wind, and drying fuels \nwill lead to elevated fire danger. Please see separate \ndiscussion for further details. \n\nSUNDAY:  Winds will weaken Saturday night as a frontal boundary \nbegins to approach from the west. However continued mixing after a \nvery warm afternoon will keep overnight low temperatures in the 60s. \nThis front will bisect the CWA by the middle of the afternoon, \npromoting another day of highs in the 80s along and southeast of the \nboundary, but a quick drop towards the 60s towards central South \nDakota.  There will be a sharp increase in mid-lvl clouds along the \nboundary which may impact heating slightly. The front slowly settles \nsoutheast into Monday morning, pulling cooler air into the James \nRiver Valley. That said, there's high potential that we'll set new \nrecord warm minimum temperatures Saturday and potentially again\nSunday.\n\nMONDAY-WEDNESDAY:  Broad ridging over the Central CONUS and the \nremnants of Hurricane Isaias will prevent any meaningful moisture to \nbe able to advect into the Tri-State area behind the passage of the \nsurface trough Monday morning. The end result may be an increase in \nmid-lvl clouds and occasional virga/sprinkles in the afternoon. \nCanadian high pressure slides southeast through the middle of the \nweek, keeping a dry forecast going, and dropping high temperatures \ncloser to their seasonal normals for a few days. \n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: A bit of uncertainty develops in the extended \nportions of the forecast as medium range models develop a deep \nupper trough over the western CONUS. By Wednesday, there seems \nto be consensus in both ensemble and AI based guidance that the \nenergy at the base of the trough begins to eject in to the \nPlains. The increasing northward moisture return combined with \nlift from the opening wave supports high PoPs from the NBM at \nthis time. EC/GEFS probabilities for >0.10\" of QPF over 24 hours\nis already over 60% through the region. While it's still a bit \nout there, the passage of this trough and eventual deepening of \na larger longwave trough through central North America could \nsignal at least a temporary shift towards colder than normal \ntemperatures into next weekend.",
